=== parameters ===
pairs: (A) Y_coin/BBB cointegrated foil; (B) Y_drift/BBB broken/non-coint foil
also report AAA/BBB as a raw-panel foil
rolling OLS 40 z_lb 20 enter |z|> 2.0 exit |z|< 0.5
costs: shared P2 model with n_legs=2 (stack + √impact)
cite: Engle–Granger two-step; MacKinnon residual-based critical values
cite: Stern pairs; Canvas m8; quants.md W13/W14 foils
further_reading: Ernest P. Chan, Algorithmic Trading..., Wiley 2013 — no quotes

=== Engle–Granger two-step (full sample) ===
step1: OLS y = alpha + beta x + u
step2: test residual u for unit root; p-values from coint() use MacKinnon EG tables
pitfall: adfuller(p) on residuals uses *plain* ADF null tables — anti-conservative vs EG
Y_coin/BBB: beta=1.2152 coint_p=0.0005188 coint_t=-4.721 crit5=-3.357 adf_plain_p=0.0002577 hand_t=-4.435
Y_drift/BBB: beta=1.3633 coint_p=0.9723 coint_t=-0.372 crit5=-3.357 adf_plain_p=0.9136 hand_t=-0.378
AAA/BBB: beta=0.1230 coint_p=0.8293 coint_t=-1.301 crit5=-3.357 adf_plain_p=0.6332 hand_t=-1.291

compare: Y_coin should reject unit root in residual (small coint_p); Y_drift / AAA often fail

=== rolling cointegration p-values (window=80) ===
Y_coin rolling p: median=0.1555 frac_p<0.05=0.355
Y_drift rolling p: median=0.6646 frac_p<0.05=0.009
pitfall: a single full-sample coint_p can look fine while rolling windows flip in/out of cointegration

=== static vs rolling hedge (Y_coin, net costs n_legs=2) ===
static  net_SR=-0.499 NAV=0.9533 turn=0.0800 OOS=-2.516
rolling net_SR=0.560 NAV=1.0344 turn=0.0433 OOS=1.743

=== shock / broken-leg note ===
After an idiosyncratic gap, static hedge leaves residual beta; rolling adapts with lag=ROLL.
|beta_roll-beta_static| mean last 60 = 0.0737

